    summary: "The signaller (Fahrdienstleiter) of Bf Neustadt in a relay interlocking: routes from a start signal to a destination for a morning half hour, a failed exit signal with the substitute signal, the fault report and the track closure for the repair team.",
    description: "The station of the lab survey and the maintenance story, as a track diagram: platform tracks G1–G3, the double-track line from Bergheim to Talsee, entry signals A and F, exit signals N1–N3 and P2, P3, points W1–W6. The interlocking refuses a route whose sections are occupied, locked by another route or closed; it moves the points, locks the route and clears the signal; the train takes the route when it may pass; the sections are released behind its tail, and a point with its section. Trains brake for a signal at stop, from its distant signal at caution until they see it clear, for the speed of the route and at their platform. At 08:13 (normal level) exit signal N2 fails: its route can be set but the signal stays at stop. A mission can let trains come late (late) and let them come earlier or later than announced (spread): a forecast is told as the neighbouring station announces it, and once more when the train is on its way. With a train dispatcher seated, the signaller takes the dispatcher's orders (a train waits for another at most so many minutes, for its passengers or to let it go first; a train that is still to come takes another platform track) and tells the dispatcher of every train as it arrives and leaves; an undertaking's wish to hold a train is then the dispatcher's to grant. Simplified from the German rules for signallers (Ril 408).",
