    id: "play-page", group: "games", title: "The play page of the big game",
    summary: "The page on which missions are played (game/play/), as a game over a picture that fills the screen: the missions as stops on a line, the one chosen with its seats (you or the computer); its turns and shifts on a stage where somebody asks each decision and what happened is read line by line; a verdict with stars. The numbers are dialogs. It knows no game by name: every game is drawn from its view.",
    description: "Three screens, each over one picture that fills the screen (the place of the mission, of the seat shown, of what a question is about; no information stands in a box on the picture: it is in the line at the top or in the rail at the side, and what is about something in the picture pulses on it). Choosing: the missions of web/game/missions/ as stops on a line, in the order of missions.json, each with its number, its name and the stars reached; a little train stands at the stop chosen, the stop to play next is marked, the arrow keys go along the line, and without a mission in the address the next one not passed is chosen. The stop chosen shows its mission on the same screen: its hook (the mission in a line or two), its facts as chips (roles, rounds, the time on the clock and the turns, solo or together), its seats as faces to take (a seat taken is the person's, the others the computer's), what a seat whose world is elsewhere must be told (the address and key of the lab's simulation), Play and Resume; its briefing, its phases, the seed and its wiring as a table open below. The rank the stars add up to leads to the logbook. Playing, as a stage, below the page's bar (which stays, for the way to the other pages); the play screen fills the window and does not scroll, a long text scrolls in its box. One line at the upper middle, without boxes (the board's blue fades out below it across the whole width; the time of a shift a thin line at the lower edge of the page's bar): in a shift the clock, its speed and a skip to the end when no person is at work, in a turn the button that ends it; the figures of the seat shown; a few words on its state (the whole sentence for screen readers). It takes a second line only when it does not fit. The mission's name and the round and phase stand in the page's bar (not on a phone; the mission's panel at the side says them too). Messages are counted by how many a game has said (`Game.dropped`, the oldest let go, and those kept), so what is new stays right in a long shift. A rail of icons at the right: the mission (its name and phases, opened beside the rail), the seats as the cast (a face per seat; when a phase begins in which no person is at work, a seat at work is shown to watch, one with a picture first), the seat's tools (its moves, its desk, taking it over or handing it to the computer), where the seat works, the messages under a bell that counts the new ones (each on its own card with its time, the new ones marked), the flows, the sound, the live picture, only the picture, and the way back to the line; one panel beside the rail at a time, closed by Escape or a click elsewhere. A new message of the seat shown comes into the line at the top, below the figures, one at a time. A phase begins with its name over the whole screen. The seat shown: its sketch if it has one (the signaller's panel, the dispatcher's district: drawn as SVG from the view's shapes; what has a menu is a button, and chosen it offers its moves below the picture; a figure about an item of the sketch, its `asset`, is not written in the line but shown on the item, and an item that needs attention, by that figure's tone or its own `alert`, pulses red or orange and can be chosen to read what is known of it), and at the bottom the talk, a band across the screen that is there while somebody speaks or asks, with the speaker at its left, taller than the band. In the band only who speaks, what is said or asked and the choices: in a turn, the game's messages not read yet, one line at a time (a person quoted in a line speaks it); then what the mission's story has somebody say; then the decision in focus, asked by its speaker as a portrait, with its choices as filled buttons numbered as their keys, a bar for its time where it has a deadline, and the other open decisions as dots to press; the railway terms in these texts are buttons that explain them. A computer's seat is shown without buttons and can be taken over, a person's handed to the computer. The desk of a seat is a dialog: its role, its moves as forms made from the view's fields, its tables and the score so far; the session's flows, to filter by flow, are another. The result: a verdict over the picture of the mission's place (the stars coming in, the score of the whole, passed or not, what was gained: a rank, badges, a best score; the cast with their scores, a question of the debrief to take along, the next stop of the line, the mission's sheet in the logbook), and the full report as a dialog: every seat's rounds and objectives, the rules broken, what led to what (every fault of the shifts on the shift's time line, each stretch of its time out of order in the colour of whose it was: a seat, the road, the team's work; what the teams did while it waited; what it did to the trains), how often each flow passed, the debrief's questions, and the session as a file. What was reached (the mission's stars and sheet, the roles sat in, the terms met) is kept in the browser as the progress, only where a seat was a person's: held by a person for at least half of its work (Session.heldByPeople), whoever holds it at the end. The session is kept in the browser after every move and can be resumed: it is played again from its record. A session that cannot be played again from its record (a seat whose world is elsewhere, the lab's simulation) is not kept, Resume is not offered for it, and the page says so before the start. When the page is left (also when the browser keeps it for \"back\", since it may throw it away later without another word), such a seat's world is given back; shown again from the cache, the mission starts anew with a note, while a session all of whose games are here goes on. Games whose world is elsewhere are synchronised between the frames.",
